===Etymology===
# From the earlier form {{term|an't|lang=en}}, a contraction of {{term|am|lang=en}} {{term|not|lang=en}}, {{term|are|lang=en}} {{term|not|lang=en}}, and {{term|is|lang=en}} {{term|not|lang=en}}. The shift from {{IPA|/ænt/|lang=en}} to {{IPA|/eɪnt/|lang=en}} parallels a similar change in some dialects with {{term|can't|lang=en}}. In other dialects the pronunciation shifted to {{IPA|/ɑːnt/|lang=en}}, and the spelling {{term|aren't|lang=en}}, when used to mean “am not”, is due to the fact that both words are pronounced {{IPA|/ɑːnt/|lang=en}} in some non-rhotic dialects. Historically, ''ain't'' was present in many dialects of the English language, but not in the southeastern England dialect that became the standard, where it is only found in the construction ''aren't I?.''
# From the earlier form {{term|han't|lang=en}}, a contraction of {{term|has|lang=en}} {{term|not|lang=en}} and {{term|have|lang=en}} {{term|not|lang=en}}. ''Han't'' shifted from {{IPA|/hænt/|lang=en}} to {{IPA|/heɪnt/|lang=en}}, and underwent h-dropping in most dialects.

